### Credentials

A credential comes from the first of: the value you pass to the constructor, for a Bearer token a token provider (`token_provider:`, called for every request), and the environment variable of its security scheme. The variable is read only when neither was passed, once, when the authenticator is built, and an empty variable counts as unset. A client whose required credential is missing is not built: the constructor raises `ArgumentError`.
{{#hasSecuritySchemeEnvVars}}

| Security scheme | Credential | Environment variable |
|---|---|---|
{{#securitySchemeEnvVars}}
| `{{schemeId}}` | `{{fieldSnake}}` | `{{envName}}` |
{{/securitySchemeEnvVars}}

An authenticator you build without a scheme (`Client.with_token`, or `BearerAuthenticator`, `BasicAuthenticator` and `ApiKeyAuthenticator` on their own) reads `{{envVarPrefix}}_TOKEN`, `{{envVarPrefix}}_USERNAME`, `{{envVarPrefix}}_PASSWORD` or `{{envVarPrefix}}_API_KEY`.
{{/hasSecuritySchemeEnvVars}}

{{#hasClientSchemes}}
### Credentials per security scheme

`{{clientClassName}}.new` takes one optional credential for each security scheme of the API, named after the scheme: {{#clientSchemes}}`{{argName}}:`{{^-last}}, {{/-last}}{{/clientSchemes}}. Each operation uses the credential of the first scheme in its own security requirement that you configured; there is no per-call override. An optional `authenticator` (the first argument) serves any operation no scheme credential matches, and an operation that declares no security sends no credential of its own. The client is refused, with an `ArgumentError`, when it gets no credential at all.

{{/hasClientSchemes}}
See [SKILLS.md](SKILLS.md) for authentication, errors, transport options and a worked example.

## Configuration

`TransportOptions.builder` sets `timeout` (milliseconds, one end-to-end budget across redirects; a non-positive value raises `ArgumentError`), `proxy`, `verify_ssl`, `ca_cert_path`, `follow_redirects`, `max_redirects`, `user_agent`, `default_headers`, `inject_request_id`, `max_retries` and `max_response_bytes`. The proxy is exactly what you configure: `HTTP_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` are ignored. Every request carries an `X-Request-ID` header unless you send one.

`TransportOptions.builder.max_response_bytes(n)` bounds a buffered response body to `n` bytes (off by default): a body that passes it, on the wire or once decompressed, fails with `ResponseTooLargeError` and is never retried. A streamed response is not bounded by it.

### Closing the client

`client.close` is graceful and idempotent. The client is marked closed at once and a request started afterwards raises a `RuntimeError` ("ApiClient has been closed and can no longer send requests"); a request already in flight finishes, and the connection the SDK built is released when the last one completes. Streams that are still open are cancelled. A second `close` does nothing, and a `Faraday::Connection` you supplied is never closed.

### Cookie parameters

A cookie parameter is sent as it is, without percent-encoding, when every character of its value is a legal RFC 6265 cookie-octet (printable ASCII except space, double quote, comma, semicolon and backslash). Any other value raises an `ArgumentError` before the request is sent, so encode the value yourself first if it holds one of those characters.

### Streaming

An operation whose success response is binary (an octet stream, an image, `type: string, format: binary`) returns a `ByteStream` instead of the whole body in memory, so a large download never has to fit in memory. The call returns as soon as the response headers arrive, and the body is read as you consume it. A `ByteStream` has the status code, the lower-cased headers and an idempotent `close` that aborts the connection instead of draining it, so close it when you stop early (`ensure`). `read_all` reads the rest into memory and leaves the stream closed.
{{#hasBinaryStreamOperation}}

```ruby file
require '{{gemName}}'
{{#binaryStreamOperation}}

def save_to(client{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, path)
  stream = client.{{#binaryStreamGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/binaryStreamGroup}}.{{operationId}}({{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}{{^-first}}, {{/-first}}{{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}})
  puts stream.status_code
  File.open(path, 'wb') { |file| stream.each { |chunk| file.write(chunk) } }
ensure
  stream&.close
end
{{/binaryStreamOperation}}
```
{{/hasBinaryStreamOperation}}

The call `timeout` covers the wait for the response headers and stops there. `idle_timeout` (in `TransportOptions`, default 60 seconds, `0` or `nil` disables it) bounds the gap between two reads of the body and raises `NetworkTimeoutError`. Retries, redirects, token refresh and the error contract apply up to the headers exactly as for a buffered call; a request is never retried after the first body byte, and a non-2xx status raises the usual typed error with its body read up to 64 KiB and never returns a stream. A connection reset, or a body that ends before its declared `Content-Length`, raises `StreamError`, a `NetworkError` with status 0. `client.close` cancels the streams that are still open, and their next read raises the closed-client error. A custom transport that does not implement `send_streaming` fails a stream call with `NotImplementedError` naming it, and its buffered calls keep working.

An operation whose request body is raw binary (`type: string, format: binary`) takes a `BodySource` instead of buffering the bytes, so a large upload never has to fit in memory. A `BodySource` is replayable, made with `BodySource.of(bytes)`, `BodySource.of_file(path)` or `BodySource.from_factory(factory)` (a factory that opens a fresh stream each time), or single use, made with `BodySource.once(stream)`. The body is sent as it is read: a known length (bytes, a file, or a length you pass) goes out as `Content-Length` and an unknown length is sent chunked. A replayable body is read again for a retry after a 5xx, a 429 or a network error and for a 307 or 308 redirect. A single-use body is never retried, so the original error is raised unchanged, and a 307 or 308 is refused with `Refusing to replay a streamed request body`; it is never buffered to make a replay possible. The SDK never closes a stream you pass to `once` and closes the streams it opens itself from a file or a factory. Multipart bodies stay buffered. A 401 that refreshes the token follows the same rule, and an event stream request with a single-use body is never reconnected.

An operation whose success response is JSON Lines (`application/x-ndjson`, `application/jsonl`, `application/jsonlines`) or server-sent events (`text/event-stream`) has no buffered method. It has a `<operation>_stream` method that takes the operation's parameters, its options object and a trailing `StreamOptions`, and returns a `JsonLinesStream` of the schema type or an `SseStream` of `ServerSentEvent` (`id`, `event`, `data`, `retry` and the raw `data` text; the `data` is decoded as the schema of the payload, or is a string). Iterate it, and close it when you stop early. A JSON Lines line that does not decode, and a line or event longer than `max_event_bytes` (1 MiB), raise `SerializationError` and end the stream.
{{#hasJsonLinesStreamOperation}}

```ruby file
require '{{gemName}}'
{{#jsonLinesStreamOperation}}

def read_feed(client{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}})
  feed = client.{{#jsonLinesStreamGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/jsonLinesStreamGroup}}.{{vendorExtensions.op.streamMethodName}}({{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}{{^-first}}, {{/-first}}{{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}})
  feed.each { |item| puts item }
ensure
  feed&.close
end
{{/jsonLinesStreamOperation}}
```
{{/hasJsonLinesStreamOperation}}
{{#hasSseStreamOperation}}

```ruby file
require '{{gemName}}'
{{#sseStreamOperation}}

def read_events(client{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}})
  events = client.{{#sseStreamGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/sseStreamGroup}}.{{vendorExtensions.op.streamMethodName}}({{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}{{^-first}}, {{/-first}}{{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}})
  events.each { |event| puts event.data }
ensure
  events&.close
end
{{/sseStreamOperation}}
```
{{/hasSseStreamOperation}}

An event stream reconnects when the connection ends, fails or stays silent for the idle timeout: it asks again with fresh headers and `Last-Event-ID`, waits the server's `retry` value or `reconnect_delay` (1 second, doubling to 30 seconds with jitter), and raises the last failure after `max_reconnects` failures in a row without an event (5; 0 for a request with a body; `-1` is unlimited). It never reconnects after a 204, a status other than 408, 429 and 5xx, a response that is not `text/event-stream`, a cancellation or `client.close`. An event named `error` raises `ApiError` with the response status and the data as its body, and a comment line such as a heartbeat counts as activity for the idle timeout. The generator option `sseTerminator` names a data value (such as `[DONE]`) that ends the stream and is not delivered. `StreamOptions` also takes `idle_timeout` and `last_event_id`.

### Pagination

An operation that returns a list one page at a time has a `<operation>_pages` method next to its plain method. It takes the same parameters in the same order and returns a `Paginator`, an `Enumerable` of the item type. The method sends no request: the first request goes out when you first read an item or a page, and each next request goes out only when you ask for more, so stopping early sends nothing further and leaves nothing to close. Iterate the paginator for the items, call `items` for an `Enumerator` of items, or call `pages` for an `Enumerator` of `Page` values with `items`, `next_cursor` and `result`, the same `ApiResult` that the `_with_http_info` variant returns, so the status and headers stay reachable. Iterating again starts again from the starting position.
{{#hasPagedOperation}}

```ruby file
require '{{gemName}}'
{{#pagedOperation}}

def read_everything(client{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}})
  list = client.{{#pagedGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/pagedGroup}}.{{vendorExtensions.op.pagesMethodName}}({{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}{{^-first}}, {{/-first}}{{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}})
  list.each { |item| puts item }
  list.pages.each { |page| puts "#{page.items.size} items, status #{page.result.status_code}" }
end
{{/pagedOperation}}
```
{{/hasPagedOperation}}

The cursor you pass to the method is the starting position, and the next cursor of a page resumes the list: pass it as the starting cursor of a new call. The page size and every other parameter, option and header are repeated unchanged on each page request, and each page is an ordinary call with its own retries, timeout, redirects, authentication and redaction. An error on a later page is raised from the read that needed it, after the items of the earlier pages were delivered, and ends the iteration; the SDK never skips a page. A list ends when the next cursor is absent, `nil` or empty, and an empty page with a next cursor continues. A page that returns the cursor that was just sent raises `PaginationError`, a direct child of `{{apiErrorParentSimpleName}}`, after its items were delivered, instead of looping. There is no total limit and no collect-all: use `first(n)`.

How each style ends and resumes: an id list passes the `last_id` or `next_cursor` of each page as the starting cursor of the next request and ends when a declared `has_more` is false. An offset list starts at the offset you pass, sends the offset past the items it received on each next request, and ends on an empty page or when a declared `has_more` is false. A page-number list sends the page you pass, or none, and then asks for the page after each one, and ends on an empty page or when a declared `has_more` is false. A next URL list takes the URL in the body of each page, follows it only within the origin of the client, and ends when the property is absent, `null` or empty. A cursor, offset or page that does not advance raises `PaginationError` as a token cursor does.

A list that is followed by a `Link` header (`rel="next"`, RFC 8288) takes the next URL from the header of each response. A next URL that leaves the scheme, host or port of the client is refused with `PaginationError` and nothing, no credentials either, is sent to it; such a method has no starting cursor, so it cannot resume.

A list whose page body carries the next URL in a property (a next URL list) is followed in the same way as a `Link` list: the URL is taken from the body of each response, a next URL that leaves the scheme, host or port of the client is refused with `PaginationError`, nothing is sent to it, and such a method cannot resume from a saved position.

The generator finds a paged operation by its names and shape. The operation must be a GET with one JSON 2xx response, and it must match exactly one of these styles. A token cursor is one query parameter among `cursor`, `after`, `page_token`, `next_token`, `continuation_token` and `marker`, paired with a next-cursor property among `next_cursor`, `next_page_token`, `next_token`, `next_marker`, `next_continuation_token`, `continuation_token` and `next` of the same type. An id cursor is one query parameter among `starting_after`, `after_id` and `after`, paired with a boolean `has_more` and a string `last_id` or `next_cursor` property. A `Link` list has a `Link` header declared on the response. An offset list has an `offset` query parameter with a `limit`, and a page-number list has a `page` query parameter with one of `per_page`, `page_size`, `size` or `limit`. A next URL list has no cursor parameter and a string property with `format: uri` or `uri-reference`, or one named `next_url`, `next_uri`, `nextlink` or `next_link`, that is optional or nullable. An operation that matches more than one style is ambiguous, and one that is streamed is never paged; either keeps only its plain method, and the generation log names it and says why. The `x-fern-pagination` and `x-speakeasy-pagination` (cursor type) extensions are read as hints. The generator option `paginationOverrides` (a JSON object that maps an operation id to `none` or to `{style, requestParam, responseProperty, itemsProperty, hasMoreProperty}`, where the style is `token`, `link`, `id`, `offset`, `page` or `body`) wins over a hint and over inference, and `pagination=off` switches the feature off.

### Custom HTTP client

`TransportOptions.builder.connection(faraday_connection)` supplies your own `Faraday::Connection`. The SDK keeps applying its timeout, user agent, redirect policy and query encoding, and never closes a connection you supplied. The SDK's own connection keeps up to 100 sockets alive between calls through `faraday-net_http_persistent`, closing each after 5 seconds idle. A connection you supply can use any Faraday adapter, and its pool is yours to manage.

### Testing your code

Every API group has an interface (`{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}_{{classname}}Interface{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}`, an RBS interface) and the client has one (`_{{clientClassName}}Interface`), so a test can replace the SDK with a hand-written fake: no mocking library, no network.

```ruby statements
# A fake is any object that responds to the operations you use; Ruby needs no base class.
fake = Class.new do
  def close; end
end.new

# Code under test takes anything that satisfies the group interface, never the generated class.
use = ->(api) { api }
use.call(fake)
```

A new operation in the API adds a method to the interface, so a fake that must satisfy the whole interface for Steep fails to type-check after you regenerate; fake only the operations your code calls, or delegate the rest to a base fake.

### Automatic retries

`TransportOptions.builder.max_retries(n)` sets how many times a failed request is sent again (default `2`; `0` turns retries off). A request is retried after a network error that happened before any response, and after a `408`, `409`, `429` or `5xx` (not `501`) response. The wait starts at 0.5 seconds and doubles up to 8 seconds, with up to 25% jitter; a `Retry-After` header (seconds or an HTTP date, at most 60 seconds) is honoured instead. The `timeout` budget is never extended by retries, a TLS failure is not retried, and a multipart body or a single-use `BodySource` is never sent twice. Every retry is a new request: its headers, authentication included, are built again.

### Per-call options

Every operation takes an optional Options object, which accepts three members that apply to that call alone: `timeout` (milliseconds, replacing the client's end-to-end timeout), `headers` (sent in addition to the default headers, and replacing a default of the same name) and `max_retries` (replacing the client's retry count). A member left `nil` keeps the client's value. A client built with your own `ApiClient` receives the headers only. An operation that has no query, header, form or cookie parameter and no security has an Options object that carries only these three members.

{{#hasExampleOperation}}
{{#exampleOperation}}
```ruby file
require '{{gemName}}'

def {{operationId}}_within_two_seconds(client)
  options = {{moduleName}}::Api::Options::{{vendorExtensions.op.optionsClassName}}.new(timeout: 2_000, headers: { 'X-Tenant' => 'a' }, max_retries: 0)
  client.{{#exampleOperationGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/exampleOperationGroup}}.{{operationId}}(options)
end
```
{{/exampleOperation}}
{{/hasExampleOperation}}

### `Configuration` and `TransportOptions`

`Configuration` holds what a request says (the base URL and the default headers) and `TransportOptions` holds how it is sent (timeouts, proxy, TLS, redirects, retries). They stay two objects on purpose: a `Configuration` is cheap to rebuild per tenant, while a `TransportOptions` owns the connection settings the client reuses.

### Errors

An `ApiError` (and every subclass, `NetworkError` included) carries `status_code`, `response_headers`, `response_body`, `error_body`, `request_method` and `request_path`, the path of the request without its query string. The message names them as `Request: GET /items/1` after the status code line.

An operation that declares an error response with a schema raises a class of its own for it: one error class per distinct schema, named after the schema with an `Error` suffix, subclassing the class of the status it is declared under (`4XX` gives `ClientError`, `5XX` gives `ServerError`, `default` gives `ApiError`). Its `error_body` is the decoded schema object, and the same status can be a different class in two operations. A body that does not match the schema never hides the real error: the status error is raised with the raw body and the decoding failure as its `cause`. `retryable?` is true for the statuses the client itself retries and `request_id` is the `x-request-id` response header. A response body that cannot be decoded raises a `SerializationError` carrying `status_code`, `response_headers`, `response_body`, `request_method` and `request_path`.

### Raw responses

Every operation has a `<operation>_with_http_info` variant returning an `ApiResult` with `status_code`, `headers` (a frozen copy, lower-cased names) and `data`.

### Cancellation

The SDK has no cancellation API of its own. Ruby's native mechanisms (`Timeout`, killing the thread) apply; a call that exceeds the request timeout fails with `NetworkTimeoutError`.

### Response compression

The SDK asks for `gzip, deflate` and decompresses the body itself; `br` and `zstd` are also advertised when the optional `brotli` and `zstd-ruby` gems are installed (the `optional` group in the `Gemfile`). A body that cannot be decoded raises an `ApiError` carrying the response's status.

### Logging

The SDK writes no log. To log requests, supply a `Faraday::Connection` with the logging middleware you want through `TransportOptions.builder.connection(...)`. Authorization headers and other credentials are never part of an error message or of `inspect`.

### Hooks and telemetry

Every operation calls one small, optional hook around the call. The hook can edit the request headers before the request is sent and observe the status or the error afterwards, and without a hook set the call does nothing extra.

A hook is any object that responds to `start(info, headers)`. `info` is a `CallInfo` with `operation_id` (exactly as the spec writes it), `http_method`, `url_template` (the spec path, for example `/items/{id}`), `url` (without query string and fragment) and `server_address` (the host, lower-case, without port or IPv6 brackets). `headers` is the final, mutable set of request headers: defaults, call headers, authentication, cookies and trace context are already in it. `start` returns an observer, an object that responds to `finish(outcome)`, or `nil`. `outcome` is a `CallOutcome` with `status_code` (0 when no response arrived), `headers` (lower-cased names, `nil` when no response arrived) and `error` (the very error the caller receives, or `nil`). Set the hook with `Configuration.builder.call_hook(hook)` or the `call_hook:` keyword of the client, and use `CallHook.chain(first, second)` to run several: they start in the order given and finish in the reverse order.

`start` runs once per operation call, however many times the transport sends the request, and `finish` runs exactly once on every way the call can end. A header the hook sets is on every send of the call, and the fresh credentials of the one replay after a 401 still replace a header of the same name. An error raised by `start` reaches the caller unchanged and nothing is sent. An error raised by `finish` is swallowed. The hook runs above the transport, so it also runs when you supply your own, and it runs inline on the calling thread, so it must not block. Headers are a plain `Hash`: reuse the spelling of an existing name to replace it.

```ruby statements
require '{{gemName}}'

tenant_hook = Class.new do
  def start(_info, headers)
    headers['X-Tenant'] = 'a'
    nil
  end
end.new

client = {{moduleName}}::{{clientClassName}}.with_token('{{{basePath}}}', 'token', call_hook: tenant_hook)
```

#### OpenTelemetry

This SDK ships no OpenTelemetry adapter and the SDK itself depends on no OpenTelemetry gem: the Ruby OpenTelemetry metrics API is not stable yet, so the same hook is shown as a recipe instead. It records one span per call, named with the operation id, of kind client, with `http.request.method`, `url.template`, `server.address`, `http.response.status_code` (only when a response arrived) and `error.type` (the status code as text for a status of 400 or more, otherwise `timeout`, `network_error`, `serialization_error`, `cancelled` or `_OTHER`). It also records the counter `http.client.request.count` (unit `{request}`) and the histogram `http.client.request.duration` (unit `s`) with the same attributes, under an instrumentation scope named after the gem and carrying its version. It injects the `traceparent` header itself, so the span sent to the server is the client span. Add `opentelemetry-api` and `opentelemetry-metrics-api` to your own `Gemfile`; without an OpenTelemetry SDK it does nothing.

```rb
{{>call_hook_recipe}}
```

```rb
client = {{moduleName}}::{{clientClassName}}.with_token('{{{basePath}}}', 'token', call_hook: TelemetryCallHook.new)
```

